WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.540
Cloud deployment scripts should be predictable and reviewable.

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:10.840
A safer Bash wrapper keeps command structure fixed, separates options from operands, and makes the deployment target visible.

3
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:16.700
Avoid building one large command string from variable data and then asking the shell to interpret it again.

4
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:25.360
That style makes quoting harder, hides behavior from reviewers, and increases the chance that an unexpected value changes what the script does.

5
00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:35.300
Building one large command string makes quoting harder, hides behavior from reviewers, and increases the chance that an unexpected value changes what the script does.

6
00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:41.420
Quote values that should remain literal, and use Bash arrays where option lists or arguments need to be assembled.

7
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:56.240
Validate account identifiers, project names, tenants, subscriptions, regions, resource names, file paths, image references, artifact versions, tags, labels, and timeout values before using them.

8
00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:07.140
Validation should favor known patterns and approved values rather than treating every argument, environment variable, or CI/CD field as a trusted deployment instruction.

9
00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:17.660
Working directory control matters because deployment packages, rendered templates, configuration files, manifests, and scripts often use relative paths.

10
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:27.200
A helper should know which repository and directory it is operating in, verify that expected files exist, and avoid letting leftover workspace state influence a later deployment.

11
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:34.820
Tool version expectations also matter because different cloud CLI, packaging, IaC, or deployment tool versions can produce different behavior.

12
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.620
Safe stopping is part of secure automation.

13
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:50.480
Scripts should check exit status, distinguish expected no-change results from failures, enforce timeouts where appropriate, and stop before later steps trust an incomplete or failed deployment.

14
00:01:50.580 --> 00:02:01.380
Error messages should help operators understand what failed without exposing secrets, raw credentials, sensitive configuration, or unnecessary resource detail.

15
00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:06.120
A good deployment wrapper is strict, boring, and easy to audit.
